s a \"Sleep Quality\" score. It uses a combination of accelerometer data from your phone and sound analysis from the microphone. Sleep Better offers personalized insights and recommendations to improve your sleep quality.\n7. **Sleep Watch** (iOS, Android): Sleep Watch tracks sleep disruptions, including awakenings, and provides a \"Sleep Quality\" score. It uses sound analysis from your phone's microphone to detect movements and awakenings. Sleep Watch offers personalized insights and recommendations to improve your sleep quality.\n\nThese apps can help you:\n\n* Track sleep disruptions, including awakenings during the night\n* Monitor sleep patterns, including duration, stages, and quality\n* Receive personalized insights and recommendations to improve sleep quality\n* Set goals and track progress towards better sleep\n* Integrate with wearable devices or health apps for a more comprehensive view of your sleep and overall health\n\nRemember to read reviews, understand the app's tracking methods, and consider your personal preferences when choosing a sleep tracking app. It's essential to find an app that fits your needs and provides accurate insights to help you improve your sleep quality.\nUser turn 11: I'll definitely try out some of these apps and see which one works best for me. I've heard that some apps can also track sleep stages, such as REM and non-REM sleep. Do any of these apps track sleep stages, and if so, how accurate are they?\nAssistant turn 12: Yes, some sleep tracking apps can track sleep stages, including REM and non-REM sleep. The accuracy of these features varies depending on the app and the technology used. Here's a breakdown of the apps I mentioned earlier and their sleep stage tracking capabilities:\n\n1. **Sleep Cycle**: Sleep Cycle tracks sleep stages, including REM and non-REM sleep, using sound analysis from your phone's microphone. Accuracy: 7/10 (dependent on microphone quality and placement)\n2. **Pillow**: Pillow tracks sleep stages, 